  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{60\\,\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{60\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{{60}^{2}\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =39.2\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=88.2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}88.2 & =(39.2)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 18(4.9) & =8(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 18\\cancel{(4.9)} & =8\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8t-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-18)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+72}}{2} \\\\  & \u2248\\frac{-8\\pm 11.6619}{2} \\\\  & \u2248-9.83,\\,1.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $1.83\\,\\text{s}.$",
